uted within each group.\n3. Homogeneity of variances: The variances among the groups should be approximately equal (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nANOVA is applicable when comparing means across multiple groups or levels of a factor. It is particularly useful when:\n- The researcher is interested in understanding the effect of one or more categorical independent variables on a continuous dependent variable.\n- The experimental design allows for the collection of data that meets the assumptions of ANOVA.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis involves the following steps:\n1. Formulate the null hypothesis (H0: all group means are equal) and the alternative hypothesis (H1: at least one group mean is different).\n2. Calculate the F-statistic using the ratio of the variance between groups to the variance within groups.\n3. Compare the calculated F-statistic to the critical value from the F-distribution table at a specified significance level (e.g., α = 0.05).\n4. If the F-statistic exceeds the critical value, reject the null hypothesis.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- ANOVA can handle multiple groups simultaneously, reducing the risk of Type I error compared to multiple t-tests.\n- It provides a comprehensive analysis of variance components, allowing for the identification of significant factors and interactions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- ANOVA is sensitive to violations of its assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances.\n- It does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests are required for pairwise comparisons.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\n- t-tests for comparing two groups.\n- Mixed-effects models for handling more complex data structures.\n- Non-parametric alternatives like Kruskal-Wallis test when assumptions of ANOVA are violated.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nResearchers should ensure that the experimental design i